[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and marked known: the parser reads dates
month first. Read report by report, it holds three problems. Each has its own ticket, and one
fix did not close the others.

<Callout kind="warn">
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is fixed (<code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales</code>)
 and the booking-v3 form is retired, so <code>hint</code> won't be fixed. Neither touches
<code>shift</code>. A one-day move reported after 5 October is still that bug: do not close it as a
duplicate of #4471.
</Callout>

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

- **`parser`**: the shared parser read every date month first, and every tenant on file
  writes the day first. Day and month swap exactly: 7 April becomes 4 July. App, email and the
  booking-v2 form. Fixed.
- **`hint`**: booking-v3 printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People typed what it said,
  and the form read it day first. Same symptom as `parser`, a different fix. Form reports from
  booking-v3 tenants only. The form is retired.
- **`shift`**: the date moves by one day, or differs between two timezones ("Lisbon got
  Tuesday, Toronto got Monday"). No swap. These are the desk's one-day moves
  (`desk-notes.json`): counted under `parser`, where they were filed, but not fixed by it.

## How a report is placed

1. Count each report once, by its id. The ticket export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
2. Look up the tenant (`src/lib/data/tenants`), not the reporter's browser. Intake's tag
   is a guess from the browser locale and disagrees with the tenant often (column "intake
   tagged otherwise").
3. A day-first tenant reporting through a booking-v3 form is `hint`. Any other channel or
   form is `parser`.
4. Read the body. If the date did not swap but moved by a day, it is `shift`: add its id to
   `oneDayMove` in `desk-notes.json`.

## Why it was one ticket

Triage merges on the subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)).
These three problems share subjects like "Wrong day on the invite", so the rule put them
together. The subject tells you the symptom. Only the tenant, the form and the body tell you
which problem it is.

